00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
crammed inside this art studio on the  outskirts of ghana's capital accra  daniel jasper is finishing up the final  touches on a work commissioned by a  foreign collector the veteran movie  poster designer is well known in ghana  for putting paintbrush to canvas over  the past 30 odd years inspired by some  of the world's most popular films  but the market for his work which once  had canadians clamoring for cinema seats  has changed  i mean bit interest more okay  people are no longer interested in going  out to watch a movie when it can be  watched from the comfort of their own  phone but there is a growing interest in  owning these hand-painted posters  internationally when someone watches a  movie and they like it they might place  an order for a poster and now they hang  them in private rooms or show them in  exhibitions  from the 1970s ghana developed a  tradition of advertising films with  these vibrant hand-painted posters  at the time local cinemas were  flourishing and artists competed over  who could entice the largest audience  with their imaginative eye-popping  designs but with the rise of the  internet the curtain came down on  ghana's independent cinemas  joseph frimpong is a university  professor in pop culture anthropology  we need to have a way of motivating  you know  upcoming artists to be rooted in this  tradition right of course they can you  know you know they can  you know move on to other art forms but  to preserve this way of painting we need  to have a way of you know motivating  these artists you know to grow  while jasper's work has gained  international appeal young artists  struggle to gain the same recognition  many turn to painting shop signs or  commissioned portraits to make a living  jasper says to keep the cinema poster  tradition alive up and coming artists  must be taught how to earn from their  work  international demand has given us the  money to sustain our careers so we teach  the younger generation so that they too  have something with which they can take  care of themselves  passing on the paintbrush for future  generations  jillian wolf al jazeera
 

